            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Apply the cosine rule on both sides of the same diagonal, use supplementary opposite angles and eliminate the diagonal’s squared length.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let the diagonal opposite $\\theta$ have length $t$. The opposite angle is $180^\\circ-\\theta$.\n\nThe cosine rule gives\n$$\\begin{align*}t^2&=a^2+b^2-2ab\\cos\\theta\\\\t^2&=c^2+d^2-2cd\\cos(180^\\circ-\\theta)\\\\t^2&=c^2+d^2+2cd\\cos\\theta.\\end{align*}$$\nEquating the expressions for $t^2$ and rearranging gives\n$$\\begin{align*}2(ab+cd)\\cos\\theta&=a^2+b^2-c^2-d^2\\\\\\cos\\theta&=\\frac{a^2+b^2-c^2-d^2}{2(ab+cd)}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
